****************************************************************************

                               Polling Door

                     A NEW-Easy-Multi-Ansi-Voting Door

                             From Peter Deane

                                Version 1.0

                           GFA-BASIC Comp V 3.51


****************************************************************************

                             What does it do?
                             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This door is a super-voting door.  It draws heavily on Adam Levin's
Multi-Vote door for file structure, but supports:

* Full ANSI
* Up to 20 Surveys
* Up to 20 Questions per Survey
* Up to 20 Responses per Question
* Much easier to setup!
* Powerful Maintenance

Remember the limits:

* 20 surveys
* 20 questions per survey
* 20 responses per question


****************************************************************************

                             Basic Door Setup
                             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

You set up a door on Metro by making an extra directory in a 'DOORFILES'
directory.

If you are running an early version of Metro, you only have 1 doorfiles
directory.

In Version 7.0c+, there are up to 5 Doorfiles directories, called
Doorfiles1- Doorfiles5.
 
Create a directory in either doorfiles or doorfilesX (as applicable)
which has a number that's (a) unique and (b) within the range for that
doorfiles directory as specified in the docs.

In this directory, the actual door executable is copied.  The exe itself
is renamed 'DOORXX' where XX is the same number as the doorXX directory
you just made.  Maybe you need support files in this directory - each
door is different.  Maybe the door will CREATE files in this directory,
which you should leave alone, unless you know what you're doing.

Make sure you put all support files in this directory with the door exe
file.

Then you add in an entry to the menu files (Doorcmds or DoorCmdsX) so
the users can access the door.


****************************************************************************


                             Making up a menu
                             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The door requires you to make up two types of files.  The surveys
themselves - but that will be discussed later - and the MENU file.

The name of the menu file is 'Poll.menu' and it will need to be in the
door directory for the polling door.  It has a VERY simple format, of
two lines per menu item.

I've included 5 example surveys you can put on pretty much as they
stand, so I'll refer to the examples.

Poll.menu


B
BS Progress Survey
C
omputers
P
olitical Opinion
S
exual Preference!
A
miga Files Survey


The first line is the keypress for that survey/poll/set_of_voting_questions.
The second line is what's printed up after the brackets.

The menus will be automatically generated for you thus:

[B]BS Progress Survey
[C]omputers
[P]olitical Opinion
[S]exual Preference!
[A]miga Files Survey

Where the keypress will be bracketed, and the colours will automatically
be selected to give a pretty menu if the ANSI option is on.


You could do the menus like this:

1
 BBS Progress Survey
2
 Computers
3
 Political Opinion
4
 Sexual Preference!
5
 Amiga Files Survey


Note to add the space in front of every second line.  This would print
up like


[1] BBS Progress Survey
[2] Computers
[3] Political Opinion
[4] Sexual Preference!
[5] Amiga Files Survey


and be easier to maintain if you get repeated keypresses as surveys are
added or removed later on.

The former method is easier for the user, the latter is easier for sysop.


********
WARNING:
********

You CANNOT use these keypresses:

[R]econfigure
[Q]uit
[*]Maintenance

Okay, do NOT use a R, Q or * in your menus!  I use them for the door and
the door will get in first, making your choice unselectable.

***********
WARNING OFF
***********


The Maintenance section will have an option to add/start/edit a menu
online!

****************************************************************************

                                Poll Files
                                ~~~~~~~~~~

For each menu entry you have, it will be necessary to make up a file
with all the questions.

You can have up to 20 polls running at the same time.

So, with our example:


[A]miga Files Survey
[B]BS Progress Survey
[C]omputers
[P]olitical Opinion
[S]exual Preference!


These files will be made up by the sysop:

(example files for all these are included in the archive)

Poll.A
Poll.B
Poll.C
Poll.P
Poll.S

The poll files are pretty easy to setup. Here's what I get you to do:

Example question:

****
Do you call the BBS as often as before ?
****
Yes - MORE often     
Yes - as often       
No  - not as often   
N/A - Fence Sitters! 

The first thing you need is a line with '*****'s on it.  You need at
least 3 *** starting at column zero.  This is just to wake the program
up to the fact that a question is coming up.

Then you need the text for the question itself.  This can be, basically
as long as you like.  Choose yourself whether to go to 40/80 columns,
etc.

When you finish the question (even if it's only 1 line), you add in
another 3 or 4 **** line.

Then you add the alternatives, and you can have up to 20 repsonses.

Here's some more of that file:


****
How do you think the BBS has improved ?
****
Love-it!             
A lot better         
Much The Same        
Who Cares?           
N/A - Fence Sitters! 
****
What feature would be most valued ?
****
Full Screen Editor   
Fido Mail            
Better ANSI          
More Doors           
More Amiga Files     
N/A - Fence Sitters! 
*****
What do you use the MOST ?
****
Public Messages        
Private Messages       
Files                  
Doors - Metro Doors    
Doors - Paragon Doors  
Textfiles              
N/A - Fence Sitters!   


Note:  For better presentation, you should pad out each line with
spaces, and make them all the same length.

Viz:
         ++-Spaces added-++
         ||              ||
         \/              \/
Textfiles                |
N/A - Fence Sitters!     |
  
           \____________/
 
           Spaces added
           as needed


But, that's not 100% required.  It DOES look better if you take the time
to pad out the menu entries 'though.


I hope you get the idea of it.  The only small hitch involved is the
end, which goes like this:


****
Do you think the sysop inputs enough into the BBS?
****
Yes - Good Effort          
Yes - Reasonable           
No  - Could do more        
No  - Could do a LOT more  
N/A - Fence Sitters!       
****
END
****


/\
 \--- See to add a row of *** and the word 'END' in block capitals at the end.

      As a result, you cannot begin a line elsewhere in the menu with 'END'
      starting in column 0. It will think that's it for the alternatives.

Logic also dictates you can't start a line with ****'s and expect it to
be directly output.  It will be regarded as a marker by the program, so
don't use that as output.

For further info, see the included examples, and then start to make up
your own.

I'm sure other sysops would appreciate it if you spread any good surveys
you've made up.  It will save us all a lot of time!

The Sysop Maintenance section allows you to make up survey files, or you
can use a text editor following the above guidelines.  Or both.

I have made it as easy as possible for you to make surveys.


****************************************************************************

                             Files it creates
                             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The program will generate a few files....

For each of the polls you have on, a 'Results.X' and a 'Voted.X' file will
be created (where X is the keypress).

The results.X files are always about 800-1024 bytes long. I know that's
slighly inefficient, but for files this small, it don't matter much - still
only 3 blocks. It'd use 2 blocks even if it were 1 byte long!

The Voted.X file is a list of all voters, so it just gets bigger and bigger
as more people vote. You can 'Un-vote' someone from the maintenance section.

In Maintenance, there is the option to remove a poll. With that option,
the results will be written to an output-file and the support files and menu
entry are then removed.


****************************************************************************

                                Maintenance
                                ~~~~~~~~~~~

You can unvote anyone, or also edit the stats for each section. I think
it's pretty easy to see from the menus.

As a SPECIAL BONUS, it's possible to edit, add to or create poll.menu files,
so you can set this up and maintain it ONLINE rather than having to use a
text editor. Mind you, a text editor will still work fine around here!

And finally, as the ultimate sevice, the actual poll.X survey files can be
created through either the door, or a text editor.

Naturally, this entire section is only available to level 9 users.


